    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most foundation crack repairs cost roughly 250 to 800 dollars per crack when a contractor seals it with epoxy or polyurethane injection, and a do-it-yourself injection kit typically costs about 50 to 130 dollars. Structural problems are a different scale: stabilizing a bowing basement wall usually runs from a few thousand dollars to over 10,000 per wall, depending on whether it gets carbon fiber straps, steel beams or wall anchors. The difference between those two bills is almost entirely about what the crack is telling you. A vertical hairline crack that weeps in spring is a sealing job; a horizontal crack, a stair-step crack in block, a crack wider than about a quarter inch or one side pushed out past the other is a structural question that deserves an engineer before anyone sells you a fix. Prices below are typical US ranges and vary widely by region and over time.
        </p>

        <div id="cost-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does It Cost to Fix a Foundation Crack?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For the most common situation, a single vertical or slightly diagonal crack in a poured concrete basement wall that leaks during heavy rain, expect a contractor to charge somewhere around 250 to 800 dollars to inject it. The low end is a short, accessible crack in an unfinished basement; the high end is a full-height crack, a wall that needs drywall or paneling removed, or a company in a high-cost metro. Many contractors have a minimum service charge, so the first crack costs more than each additional one on the same visit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Costs climb once a crack signals movement. Repairs that add strength, such as carbon fiber reinforcement across a crack, steel beams on a bowing wall or anchors buried in the yard, are priced per strap, beam or anchor and add up to thousands per wall. Settlement problems that need piers under the footing are larger still, usually priced per pier and often running well into five figures for a whole side of a house. Exterior excavation to seal or rebuild a wall sits at the top of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below gives typical US ranges for the repairs you are most likely to be quoted. Use it to sanity-check a bid, not as a price list. Labor costs vary a lot by region, material prices move, and the condition of your particular wall can push any job outside these ranges. If a quote is far above them, ask what is driving the difference; if it is far below for a structural repair, ask what is being left out.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Repair</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Fixes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY injection kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 50 to 130 dollars per kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One non-structural crack, roughly 4 to 10 ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pro epoxy or polyurethane injection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 250 to 800 dollars per crack</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leaking or cosmetic cracks in poured walls</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbon fiber straps</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 400 to 1,000 dollars per strap</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Early bowing, under about 2 in of deflection</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Steel I-beam braces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 500 to 1,200 dollars per beam</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate bowing, can be tightened over time</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wall anchors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 600 to 1,200 dollars per anchor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bowing walls, can pull the wall back gradually</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Structural engineer inspection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 300 to 1,000 dollars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Independent diagnosis and repair spec</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wall excavation and rebuild</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 15,000 dollars and up per wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Severe bowing, failed or collapsing walls</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Keep in mind that the crack itself is usually a symptom. Gutters dumping water at the foundation, soil sloping toward the house and poor drainage cause the hydrostatic pressure that opens and pushes on walls. A repair that ignores the water source often has to be repeated. Budgeting a little for downspout extensions, grading or a drainage fix alongside the crack repair is usually money well spent, and our guide to <a href="/hydrostatic-pressure-in-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">hydrostatic pressure in basements</a> explains why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="epoxy-vs-poly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Epoxy or Polyurethane Injection: Which One Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both methods work the same way on the surface. The contractor cleans the crack, glues small injection ports along it, seals the face of the crack with a paste so the material cannot run out, and then pumps resin into the ports under low pressure starting from the bottom until the crack is filled through the wall's thickness. The difference is the resin, and the right choice depends on whether the crack is wet and whether you need strength back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy cures into a rigid material that bonds to concrete and is often stronger than the concrete around it. That makes it the choice when you want to restore structural continuity to a crack that has stopped moving. It is slower to cure, it needs a relatively dry crack to bond well, and because it is rigid, a crack that is still moving may simply open again beside the repair. Some contractors charge slightly more for epoxy because of the material and the slower working time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polyurethane is the leak-stopper. It reacts with water, expands into a flexible foam and fills the crack even when water is actively seeping through it. Because it stays somewhat flexible, it tolerates the small seasonal movement many foundation cracks have. It does not add strength to the wall, so it is the wrong tool if the crack is structural, but it is often the best choice for a typical wet basement crack whose only problem is water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pricing for the two is close enough that you should pick on suitability, not cost. A reputable contractor should be able to explain why they chose one over the other for your crack. If you are only getting one option, ask about the other. And keep in mind that injection works in poured concrete walls; in concrete block walls, cracks typically follow mortar joints and the hollow cores make injection unreliable, so block walls usually get different treatment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="structural" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is My Foundation Crack Cosmetic or Structural?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most cracks in poured concrete basement walls are not structural. Concrete shrinks as it cures, and thin vertical or slightly diagonal cracks, often near windows or in the middle of long walls, are common in houses only a few years old. If the crack is hairline to roughly an eighth of an inch, the two sides line up flush, and it is not getting wider, the main concern is usually water and soil gas getting through, not the wall's strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            Several signs change that picture and mean you should get a professional opinion. Horizontal cracks, especially partway up a wall, usually indicate soil pressure pushing the wall inward. Stair-step cracks along the mortar joints of a block wall can mean settling or lateral pressure. Any crack wider than about a quarter inch deserves a closer look, as does any crack where one side has shifted in or out relative to the other, which is called displacement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for supporting evidence elsewhere. Walls that visibly lean or bow when you sight along them, gaps between the wall and the floor joists, doors and windows upstairs that suddenly stick, new cracks in drywall above the foundation and sloping floors all suggest movement. A single sign is not proof, but several together mean the crack is likely part of a structural problem, and sealing it alone will not address the cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest diagnostic tool is time. Mark the ends of the crack with a pencil line and the date, and measure the width at a few marked points. Recheck every few weeks through a wet season and a freeze. A crack that stays put is very likely stable. One that grows longer or wider is active. Our guide to <a href="/basement-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement crack repair</a> covers what each crack pattern usually means in more detail.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Horizontal, wide, displaced or growing means call an engineer</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A vertical hairline crack that only leaks is a sealing job. A crack that is horizontal, stair-stepped, wider than about 1/4 inch, offset, or getting worse needs a diagnosis before a repair.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="bowing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Does Bowing Basement Wall Repair Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bowing walls are where foundation repair gets expensive, and the price depends heavily on how far the wall has moved. The three common methods are carbon fiber straps, steel I-beam braces and wall anchors. Each is priced per unit, and a typical basement wall needs one every few feet, so the total depends on the wall's length as much as the method. A complete stabilization of one wall commonly lands somewhere between a few thousand and well over 10,000 dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon fiber straps are epoxied vertically to the wall, usually every four to six feet, and are typically the least expensive and least disruptive option. They stop further movement but do not push the wall back, and they are generally recommended only for walls with limited bowing, often cited as under about two inches. They are thin enough to finish over, which makes them popular in basements that will become living space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Steel I-beam braces are bolted to the floor and to the joists above and stand against the wall. They handle more deflection than carbon fiber, and many systems can be tightened periodically to nudge the wall back toward plumb over time. They take up a few inches of floor space and are harder to hide behind finished walls. Wall anchors use steel rods run through the wall to plates buried in the yard; tightening them over seasons can gradually straighten the wall, but they need clear space outside.
          </p>

          <p>
            When a wall has moved too far, typically several inches, or is cracking and shifting at the top or bottom, bracing it may no longer be adequate, and partial or full excavation and rebuilding becomes the recommendation. That is the most expensive route, often 15,000 dollars or more per wall before landscaping and finishing. An independent engineer's report is the best protection against both under-fixing a dangerous wall and overpaying for a rebuild you do not need.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Wall Total</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbon fiber straps</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minor bowing, finished basements</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 2,000 to 6,000 dollars</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Steel I-beam braces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate bowing, room inside</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 3,000 to 10,000 dollars</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wall anchors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bowing with yard access</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 4,000 to 12,000 dollars</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Excavate and rebuild</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Severe movement or failure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 15,000 dollars and up</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="engineer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Do You Need a Structural Engineer, and What Does It Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a vertical hairline leak, no. For anything that looks structural, yes, and it is one of the best-value checks in home repair. A licensed structural engineer's visit and written report typically costs roughly 300 to 1,000 dollars depending on your area and how detailed the report needs to be. That fee buys a diagnosis from someone who does not profit from selling you the repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            That independence matters because many foundation repair companies offer free inspections. A free inspection is not necessarily dishonest, but it is a sales visit, and the person doing it may be paid on what they sell. An engineer will tell you whether the wall needs repair at all, which method fits, how many braces or anchors are reasonable and whether drainage changes are part of the fix. You can then hand that specification to several contractors and compare like with like.
          </p>

          <p>
            An engineer's report also has value beyond the repair. Buyers and their inspectors are understandably nervous about foundation cracks, and a report showing the problem was diagnosed and repaired to an engineer's specification can make a future sale much smoother. Some jurisdictions require engineered plans and a permit for structural foundation work, and lenders or insurers may ask for documentation after a significant repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you call, ask whether the engineer does residential foundation evaluations regularly, what the report will include, and whether they will return to inspect the finished work. A follow-up visit may cost extra but confirms the contractor did what was specified. Keep the report, the contractor's invoice and any warranty together, since transferable warranties are a selling point only if you can produce the paperwork.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can You Repair a Foundation Crack Yourself?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a non-structural crack in a poured concrete wall, yes, and the savings are real. A two-part epoxy or polyurethane injection kit typically costs about 50 to 130 dollars and handles a single crack up to roughly the length the kit specifies, commonly 4 to 10 feet. The process is the same as the professional version on a smaller scale: clean the crack, set the ports, seal the surface, then inject from the bottom port up until resin appears at the next port.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that injection is less forgiving than it looks. If the surface seal fails, resin leaks out instead of filling the crack. If you inject too fast, the crack does not fill through the wall's thickness. Epoxy kits need a reasonably dry crack, while polyurethane kits handle damp ones. Read the kit's temperature and moisture limits, take your time and expect the first crack to go slower than the instructions suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surface products have a narrower role. Caulk and patching compounds can fill a crack cosmetically and slow minor seepage, but they sit on the surface and water pressure from behind can push them off. Our guide to <a href="/caulk-for-basement-wall-cracks/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">caulk for basement wall cracks</a> covers where that works. Once a crack is filled, a penetrating sealer or masonry waterproofer can reduce general dampness through the wall; our <a href="/best-foundation-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best foundation sealer</a> picks cover those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave structural repairs to professionals. Installing carbon fiber, beams or anchors on a wall that is actively moving takes engineering judgment, and getting it wrong can be dangerous. Do not try to push a bowing wall back yourself with jacks or by tightening improvised braces. The same goes for any crack you are not sure about: a few hundred dollars for an engineer is cheap compared with fixing the wrong problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quotes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Drives the Price, and How Do You Get a Fair Quote?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The main price drivers are the number and length of cracks, wall type, access and whether the wall is finished. Removing and replacing drywall, paneling, insulation or built-ins can add more than the repair itself. Exterior work adds excavation, which depends on depth, soil and whether a patio, deck or landscaping is in the way. Regional labor rates, permit requirements and how busy contractors are after a wet season all move the number too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for hidden costs that a low quote might omit. Ask whether the price includes moving belongings, removing finishes, cleanup, disposal, permits and any drainage work the engineer recommended. Ask what happens if a crack turns out to be longer than visible once finishes come off. For bowing walls, ask how many units are included and whether periodic tightening visits are part of the price or billed separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get at least three quotes, ideally to the same written scope, such as an engineer's report or a clear description of each crack and the method. Ask each contractor what the warranty covers, whether it is transferable to a future buyer, and whether it covers leaks, movement or both. Lifetime warranties on crack injection are common but usually cover only the repaired crack, not new ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, deal with the water. Most foundation cracks leak because water collects against the wall, and many walls bow because saturated soil pushes on them. Clean gutters, extend downspouts, regrade soil away from the house and consider drainage where water collects. If a contractor or engineer suggests a drain, our guide to <a href="/french-drain-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">french drain cost</a> shows what that typically adds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a horizontal crack like a leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A horizontal crack in a basement wall usually means soil pressure is pushing the wall inward. Injecting it to stop water does nothing about that force, and the wall can keep moving behind a neat repair. Have an engineer look at any horizontal crack, especially one with bowing or an offset between the two sides.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying only on a free inspection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Free inspections from repair companies are sales calls, and the inspector may be paid on what is sold. They can be accurate, but you have no independent check. For anything structural, spend a few hundred dollars on a licensed engineer first, then ask contractors to quote that specification so you can compare bids fairly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the water source</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gutters overflowing at the foundation, short downspouts and soil sloping toward the house create the pressure that opens cracks and bows walls. Sealing a crack without fixing those causes often leads to new cracks or a repeat repair. Address drainage at the same time, since it is usually the cheapest part of the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using epoxy on an active leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy needs a relatively dry crack to bond and cures rigid, so it is a poor match for a crack with water running through it or ongoing movement. Polyurethane expands in the presence of water and stays flexible. Choose the resin for the crack's condition, not because one sounds stronger, and ask your contractor to explain the choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not documenting the repair</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Keep photos before and after, the engineer's report, invoices and warranty documents in one place. Buyers and inspectors will ask about any visible foundation repair, and good records turn a red flag into a documented fix. Transferable warranties are only useful if you can show the paperwork and the original installer is still in business.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are vertical foundation cracks normal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Thin vertical cracks in poured concrete walls are very common and are usually caused by shrinkage as the concrete cured. If they are hairline to about an eighth of an inch, flush on both sides and not growing, they are usually not structural. They can still let in water and soil gas, which is why many homeowners seal them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does homeowners insurance cover foundation crack repair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not when the cause is settling, shrinkage, soil pressure or gradual wear, which most policies exclude. Coverage may apply if the damage results from a covered sudden event, such as a burst pipe, depending on your policy. Read your policy and talk to your agent before assuming either way, since wording varies between insurers and states.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does foundation crack injection last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly done injection on a stable crack typically lasts for the life of the wall, which is why many contractors offer long or lifetime warranties on the repaired crack. Failures usually come from poor surface sealing, incomplete filling or a crack that is still moving. New cracks elsewhere in the wall are not covered by that repair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can foundation cracks be repaired from the inside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most crack injection is done from the inside, and it works well because the resin fills the crack through the wall's full thickness. Bracing methods like carbon fiber and steel beams are also interior. Exterior work is generally reserved for full waterproofing, wall rebuilding or situations where the inside cannot be accessed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a stair-step crack in a block wall serious?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be. Stair-step cracks along mortar joints may come from minor settling or from lateral soil pressure, and the difference matters. If the crack is wider than about a quarter inch, the blocks have shifted, or the wall is bowing, get an engineer's opinion. Thin, stable stair-step cracks can often be repointed and monitored.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a foundation crack affect selling my house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It often comes up in inspection. A small, sealed shrinkage crack rarely kills a sale, but buyers may ask for documentation or a credit. Structural cracks can affect financing and price if they are not addressed. An engineer's report plus records of a completed repair, ideally with a transferable warranty, usually reassures buyers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should I worry about a foundation crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Worry when the crack is horizontal, wider than about a quarter inch, offset from one side to the other, stair-stepped through a block wall or growing over time. Supporting signs like bowing walls, sticking doors and new drywall cracks upstairs raise the concern. In those cases, call a structural engineer before booking any repair.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

